<problem>
<!-- <title>quotient rule</title> -->

<setup>

<math name="poly_ratio" simplify="numbersPreserveOrder">x^2/(1-x^2)</math>
<derivative name="d_poly_ratio" variables="x" symbolic="true">$poly_ratio</derivative>
<math name="d_poly_ratio_3" simplify="full">$$d_poly_ratio(3)</math>
<math name="exp_ratio" simplify="numbersPreserveOrder">(1-e^x)/(1+e^x)</math>
<derivative name="d_exp_ratio" variables="x" symbolic="true">$exp_ratio</derivative>
<math name="d_exp_ratio_0" simplify="full">$$d_exp_ratio(0)</math>

<math name="hPrimePretty">2x/(1-x^2)^2</math>
<math name="gPrimePretty">-2e^x / (1+e^x)^2</math>
</setup>

<!-- testing -->
<!-- <p>sol1 = <boolean>$hPrimePretty = $d_poly_ratio</boolean></p>
<p>sol2 = <boolean>$gPrimePretty = $d_exp_ratio</boolean></p> -->
<!-- /testing -->
  
Calculate the following derivatives.

<ol>

<li>

<p><m>\displaystyle h(x)=$poly_ratio </m>
</p>
<p>

<m>h'(x) =</m> 
<answer>
<award>$d_poly_ratio </award>
</answer>
</p>

  
<hint>
Some terms should cancel when you simplify your expression, making it less annoying to type than it may first seem.
</hint>

<p>
<m>h'(3)=</m> 
<answer>
<award>$d_poly_ratio_3 </award>
</answer>

</p>

</li>

<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle g(x)=$exp_ratio </m>
</p>
<p>

<m>\displaystyle \frac{ dg }{ dx }=</m> 
<answer>
<award>$d_exp_ratio </award>
</answer>
</p>

  
<hint>
Again, some terms should cancel when you simplify your expression, making it less annoying to type than it may first seem.
</hint>
  
<p>
<m>\displaystyle \frac{ dg }{ dx }\bigg|_{x=0}=</m> 
<answer>
<award>$d_exp_ratio_0 </award>
</answer>
</p>


</li>


</ol>


<solution>


<ol>

<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle h'(x)= $hPrimePretty</m>
</p>
<p>

<m>h'(3)=$d_poly_ratio_3 </m>. 
</p>
</li>

<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle \frac{ dg }{ dx }=  $gPrimePretty</m>
</p>
<p>

<m>\displaystyle \frac{ dg }{ dx }\bigg|_{x=0}= $d_exp_ratio_0 .</m>
</p>
</li>


</ol>

</solution>


</problem>